From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Pet0203 <Pet0203@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 19 Nov 2026 12:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] vboot: add a firmware disk unlock

Read disk keys out of the Cr50 and hand them to the kernel, so that an
encrypted root can be opened without an initramfs.

The keys sit in an NV index whose policy is PolicyPCR over PCR0, PCR1 and
PCR12, followed by PolicyAuthValue. The index therefore opens only in
normal mode (PCR0), on this machine (PCR1), before the cap (PCR12), and
only to someone who knows the PIN. Developer mode, recovery mode, a
different board or an already-booted OS each present different PCRs and get
nothing.

The PIN is not sent to the TPM. It is hashed with a domain string into the
index's authValue and used as an HMAC key. The reply is HMACed as well and
checked, so the keys cannot be substituted by something else driving the
bus.

PCR12 is extended on every path out of this code, including the failures.
By the time the kernel starts the index is unreadable, so root on the
running system cannot read it again until the next boot.

The handover block reuses the layout the kernel already has for kdump's
dm-crypt keys. It is published both as a /reserved-memory node with no-map
and as a /chosen property, "linux,dm-boot-keys": four cells, a 64-bit
address then a 64-bit length. no-map matters: without it the kernel is free
to hand those pages to something else before the keys have been wiped.

The prompt is a drawn screen, like depthcharge's own recovery and developer
screens: ui_draw_text() puts font bitmaps on the framebuffer. printf() does
not reach the panel, because depthcharge binds no video console. The screen
is redrawn on every keystroke so a row of asterisks grows as the PIN is
typed, a refused PIN says so on the screen, and the panel is cleared before
the kernel starts. On asurada this needs coreboot to have brought the
display up (see the coreboot patch in this repository).

PIN entry accepts only VB_KEY_FLAG_TRUSTED_KEYBOARD, which excludes USB
keyboards.

Limits, stated plainly:

 - The key block crosses the Cr50's SPI bus unencrypted (no parameter
   encryption on the session). A probe on those lines during an unlock
   records the keys.
 - Guessing is not rate limited by the TPM. The Cr50 ships with
   dictionary-attack lockout disabled, and on the unit this was developed
   on (RW 0.5.241) it did not keep lockout parameters across a reboot. The
   three tries per boot here only bound typos, so the PIN has to be strong
   enough to survive guessing at reboot speed.
 - VB_KEY_FLAG_TRUSTED_KEYBOARD also admits the serial console, which on
   these machines is the CCD debug UART behind a debug cable.
 - The NV index is defined in the owner hierarchy, whose authorisation is
   left empty, so root on the running system can delete or rewrite it.
   That is denial of service, not a way to read the keys.

Nothing calls this yet; the next patch does.
---
 src/vboot/Makefile.inc |   1 +
 src/vboot/unlock.c     | 443 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 src/vboot/unlock.h     |  34 ++++
 3 files changed, 478 insertions(+)
 create mode 100644 src/vboot/unlock.c
 create mode 100644 src/vboot/unlock.h

diff --git a/src/vboot/Makefile.inc b/src/vboot/Makefile.inc
index 4201f7a..d820aa0 100644
--- a/src/vboot/Makefile.inc
+++ b/src/vboot/Makefile.inc
@@ -14,6 +14,7 @@
 subdirs-y := callbacks crossystem nvdata stages ui util
 
 depthcharge-y += stages.c
+depthcharge-y += unlock.c
 depthcharge-y += boot_policy.c
 depthcharge-y += firmware_id.c
 depthcharge-y += nvdata.c
diff --git a/src/vboot/unlock.c b/src/vboot/unlock.c
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..93df37b
--- /dev/null
+++ b/src/vboot/unlock.c
@@ -0,0 +1,443 @@
+// SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0
+/*
+ * Firmware unlock: take the disk keys out of the Cr50 and hand them to the
+ * kernel, so root and home can be encrypted with no initramfs.
+ *
+ * The keys live in an NV index whose policy is PolicyPCR over PCR0 (boot
+ * mode), PCR1 (HWID) and PCR12, followed by PolicyAuthValue. That combination
+ * is what makes the release conditional: the index opens only in normal mode,
+ * on this machine, before PCR12 has been capped, and only to someone who knows
+ * the PIN. Developer mode, recovery mode, another board or a booted OS all
+ * present different PCRs and get nothing.
+ *
+ * The PIN is never sent to the TPM. It is hashed into the index's authValue
+ * and used as an HMAC key, so what crosses the bus is a proof rather than a
+ * secret. The reply is HMACed too and checked here, so the keys cannot be
+ * substituted by anything else that reaches the SPI lines.
+ *
+ * The reply is not encrypted, though. The session uses no parameter
+ * encryption, so the key block itself crosses the bus in the clear, and a
+ * probe on the TPM's SPI lines during an unlock can record it.
+ *
+ * PCR12 is extended before we hand over, on every path including failure. The
+ * kernel therefore starts with the index already unreadable, and no amount of
+ * root on the running system gets a second look at it until the next boot.
+ *
+ * The handover block is the same layout the kernel already uses for its kdump
+ * dm-crypt keys (struct keys_header / struct dm_crypt_key), so the kernel side
+ * stays close to code that is already upstream.
+ */
+
+#include <endian.h>
+#include <libpayload.h>
+#include <stdint.h>
+#include <string.h>
+#include <vb2_api.h>
+#include <vboot_api.h>
+
+#include "base/device_tree.h"
+#include "drivers/video/display.h"
+#include "base/list.h"
+#include "vboot/ui.h"
+#include "vboot/unlock.h"
+#include "vboot/util/memory.h"
+#include "tlcl.h"
+
+/*
+ * Matches struct dm_crypt_key in the kernel's kernel/crash_dump_dm_crypt.c and
+ * the copy of it in drivers/md/dm-boot-keys.c. Keep them in step.
+ */
+#define UNLOCK_KEY_DESC_MAX	128
+#define UNLOCK_KEY_SIZE_MAX	256
+
+struct unlock_key {
+	uint32_t key_size;
+	char key_desc[UNLOCK_KEY_DESC_MAX];
+	uint8_t data[UNLOCK_KEY_SIZE_MAX];
+};
+
+struct unlock_keys_header {
+	uint32_t total_keys;
+	struct unlock_key keys[];
+};
+
+/*
+ * Domain separation: the authValue is a hash of this string and the PIN, not
+ * of the PIN alone, so the same PIN typed into something else does not produce
+ * this machine's authValue. swifty-enroll uses the identical string; if these
+ * ever disagree the unlock fails with nothing to see.
+ */
+#define UNLOCK_DOMAIN		"swifty-unlock-v1"
+
+/* PCR 0, 1 and 12, as a bitmap of PCR index. */
+#define UNLOCK_PCR_BITMAP	((1 << 0) | (1 << 1) | (1 << 12))
+#define UNLOCK_PCR_CAP		12
+
+#define UNLOCK_PIN_MAX		64
+
+/*
+ * One typo should not cost a reboot. This is not rate limiting. The Cr50
+ * ships with dictionary-attack lockout disabled, and the one this was
+ * developed on (RW 0.5.241) did not keep lockout parameters across a reboot,
+ * so the number of guesses is bounded only by how fast the machine can be
+ * restarted. Choose the PIN with that in mind.
+ */
+#define UNLOCK_PIN_TRIES	3
+
+typedef struct {
+	uint64_t start;
+	uint64_t size;
+	DeviceTreeFixup fixup;
+} UnlockKeys;
+
+static UnlockKeys unlock_keys;
+
+/*
+ * Publish the block twice over: as a reserved-memory node so the kernel keeps
+ * its hands off the pages, and as a /chosen property so it knows where to look.
+ * no-map matters -- without it the kernel may map the range cacheably for its
+ * own use and the keys would be read out of a page that has been handed to
+ * something else.
+ */
+static int unlock_dt_fixup(DeviceTreeFixup *fixup, DeviceTree *tree)
+{
+	UnlockKeys *keys = container_of(fixup, UnlockKeys, fixup);
+	DeviceTreeNode *reserved, *node, *chosen;
+	const char *chosen_path[] = { "chosen", NULL };
+	u32 addr_cells = 1, size_cells = 1;
+	u32 *cells;
+
+	if (!keys->size)
+		return 0;
+
+	reserved = dt_init_reserved_memory_node(tree);
+	if (!reserved)
+		return 1;
+	dt_read_cell_props(reserved, &addr_cells, &size_cells);
+
+	node = xzalloc(sizeof(*node));
+	node->name = "dm-boot-keys";
+	list_insert_after(&node->list_node, &reserved->children);
+	dt_add_reg_prop(node, &keys->start, &keys->size, 1,
+			addr_cells, size_cells);
+	dt_add_bin_prop(node, "no-map", NULL, 0);
+
+	chosen = dt_find_node(tree->root, chosen_path, NULL, NULL, 1);
+	if (!chosen)
+		return 1;
+
+	/*
+	 * Four cells: a 64-bit address then a 64-bit length, regardless of
+	 * what the root node's cell counts happen to be. A single u32 would
+	 * have truncated silently the day the block landed above 4 GiB, and
+	 * the kernel side needs the length to know how much to wipe.
+	 */
+	cells = xmalloc(sizeof(*cells) * 4);
+	cells[0] = htobel((u32)(keys->start >> 32));
+	cells[1] = htobel((u32)keys->start);
+	cells[2] = htobel((u32)(keys->size >> 32));
+	cells[3] = htobel((u32)keys->size);
+	dt_add_bin_prop(chosen, "linux,dm-boot-keys", cells,
+			sizeof(*cells) * 4);
+
+	return 0;
+}
+
+/*
+ * Reserve a page for the block. It is deliberately not on the heap: the heap
+ * is handed back to the kernel as ordinary memory, and these bytes have to
+ * survive until the kernel has copied them out and wiped them.
+ */
+static struct unlock_keys_header *unlock_alloc_block(size_t size)
+{
+	void *block;
+
+	size = ALIGN_UP(size, 4096);
+	block = memalign(4096, size);
+	if (!block)
+		return NULL;
+	memset(block, 0, size);
+
+	unlock_keys.start = (uintptr_t)block;
+	unlock_keys.size = size;
+	unlock_keys.fixup.fixup = &unlock_dt_fixup;
+	list_insert_after(&unlock_keys.fixup.list_node, &device_tree_fixups);
+	memory_mark_used(unlock_keys.start, unlock_keys.start + size);
+
+	return block;
+}
+
+/* authValue = SHA256(domain || pin). The digest gives the HMAC a full-length
+ * key whatever the PIN's length, but it adds no entropy: a short PIN is as
+ * guessable as it looks. See UNLOCK_PIN_TRIES. */
+static vb2_error_t unlock_auth_value(const char *pin, uint8_t *out)
+{
+	uint8_t buf[sizeof(UNLOCK_DOMAIN) - 1 + UNLOCK_PIN_MAX];
+	size_t pin_len = strnlen(pin, UNLOCK_PIN_MAX);
+	vb2_error_t rv;
+
+	memcpy(buf, UNLOCK_DOMAIN, sizeof(UNLOCK_DOMAIN) - 1);
+	memcpy(buf + sizeof(UNLOCK_DOMAIN) - 1, pin, pin_len);
+	rv = vb2_digest_buffer(buf, sizeof(UNLOCK_DOMAIN) - 1 + pin_len,
+			       VB2_HASH_SHA256, out, VB2_SHA256_DIGEST_SIZE);
+	memset(buf, 0, sizeof(buf));
+
+	return rv;
+}
+
+/*
+ * A real screen, drawn the way depthcharge draws its recovery and developer
+ * screens: bitmaps from the font archive onto the framebuffer coreboot set up.
+ *
+ * printf() is not an option here. depthcharge binds no video console -- it
+ * initialises serial and cbmem and leaves it there, because its own UI draws
+ * rather than prints -- so anything printed reaches the CCD UART and nothing
+ * else. The panel is also dark until somebody asks for it: coreboot turns the
+ * backlight OFF in configure_backlight_and_bridge() and never back on, and
+ * board_backlight_update() in depthcharge is the only thing that raises
+ * GPIO_AP_EDP_BKLTEN again.
+ */
+
+#define UNLOCK_TITLE_H		42
+#define UNLOCK_BODY_H		28
+#define UNLOCK_NOTE_H		22
+#define UNLOCK_TEXT_FLAGS	(PIVOT_H_CENTER | PIVOT_V_TOP)
+
+static int unlock_screen_up;
+
+static void unlock_screen_begin(void)
+{
+	if (display_init()) {
+		printf("unlock: no display; the prompt is on serial only\n");
+		return;
+	}
+	backlight_update(1);
+	enable_graphics_buffer();
+	unlock_screen_up = 1;
+}
+
+/* Redrawn on every keystroke, so the row of dots grows as the PIN is typed. */
+static void unlock_screen_draw(size_t pin_len, const char *note)
+{
+	char mask[UNLOCK_PIN_MAX + 1];
+	int32_t y = UI_SCALE / 4;
+	size_t i;
+
+	if (!unlock_screen_up)
+		return;
+
+	clear_screen(&ui_color_bg);
+
+	ui_draw_text("swifty", UI_SCALE / 2, y, UNLOCK_TITLE_H,
+		     &ui_color_bg, &ui_color_fg, UNLOCK_TEXT_FLAGS, 0);
+	y += UNLOCK_TITLE_H * 2;
+	ui_draw_text("Enter the disk unlock PIN", UI_SCALE / 2, y,
+		     UNLOCK_BODY_H, &ui_color_bg, &ui_color_fg,
+		     UNLOCK_TEXT_FLAGS, 0);
+	y += UNLOCK_BODY_H * 3;
+
+	for (i = 0; i < pin_len && i < sizeof(mask) - 1; i++)
+		mask[i] = '*';
+	mask[i] = '\0';
+	/* Something has to be drawn, or an empty line looks like a hang. */
+	ui_draw_text(i ? mask : "_", UI_SCALE / 2, y, UNLOCK_BODY_H,
+		     &ui_color_bg, &ui_color_fg, UNLOCK_TEXT_FLAGS, 0);
+	y += UNLOCK_BODY_H * 3;
+
+	if (note)
+		ui_draw_text(note, UI_SCALE / 2, y, UNLOCK_NOTE_H,
+			     &ui_color_bg, &ui_color_fg, UNLOCK_TEXT_FLAGS, 0);
+
+	flush_graphics_buffer();
+}
+
+/* Hand the panel back the way it was found, so the kernel starts from a known
+ * state rather than from whatever the last frame happened to be. */
+static void unlock_screen_end(void)
+{
+	if (!unlock_screen_up)
+		return;
+	clear_screen(&ui_color_bg);
+	flush_graphics_buffer();
+	disable_graphics_buffer();
+	unlock_screen_up = 0;
+}
+
+/*
+ * Read the PIN from the internal keyboard only. VB_KEY_FLAG_TRUSTED_KEYBOARD
+ * excludes USB keyboards, which a passer-by can plug in. Note it does admit
+ * the UART, i.e. the CCD debug console: that is ChromeOS's definition, and it
+ * is only reachable with the debug cable attached. A CCD password would close
+ * that; nothing here sets one.
+ */
+static int unlock_prompt_pin(char *pin, size_t pin_max, const char *note)
+{
+	size_t len = 0;
+
+	printf("\nPIN: ");
+	unlock_screen_draw(0, note);
+
+	while (1) {
+		uint32_t flags = 0;
+		uint32_t c = VbExKeyboardReadWithFlags(&flags);
+
+		if (!c)
+			continue;
+		if (!(flags & VB_KEY_FLAG_TRUSTED_KEYBOARD))
+			continue;	/* not the internal keyboard */
+
+		if (c == '\r' || c == '\n') {
+			pin[len] = '\0';
+			printf("\n");
+			return len ? 0 : -1;
+		}
+		if (c == '\b' || c == 0x7f) {
+			if (len)
+				len--;
+			unlock_screen_draw(len, note);
+			continue;
+		}
+		if (c == 0x1b)		/* escape: give up */
+			return -1;
+		if (c < ' ' || c > '~')
+			continue;
+		if (len + 1 >= pin_max)
+			continue;
+		pin[len++] = (char)c;
+		unlock_screen_draw(len, note);
+	}
+}
+
+/* Drive the policy to the value the index was sealed under, then read it. */
+static uint32_t unlock_read_index(uint32_t index, const uint8_t *auth,
+				  void *out, uint32_t length)
+{
+	uint8_t nonce_tpm[VB2_SHA256_DIGEST_SIZE];
+	uint32_t session;
+	uint32_t rv, rv_flush;
+
+	rv = TlclStartAuthSession(&session, nonce_tpm);
+	if (rv != TPM_SUCCESS) {
+		printf("unlock: StartAuthSession failed (0x%x)\n", rv);
+		return rv;
+	}
+
+	/* Name the step that failed. A wrong PIN and a broken exchange both
+	 * end the same way from the outside, and telling them apart without
+	 * this took a firmware build and a flash. */
+	rv = TlclPolicyPCR(session, UNLOCK_PCR_BITMAP);
+	if (rv != TPM_SUCCESS)
+		printf("unlock: PolicyPCR failed (0x%x)\n", rv);
+	if (rv == TPM_SUCCESS) {
+		rv = TlclPolicyAuthValue(session);
+		if (rv != TPM_SUCCESS)
+			printf("unlock: PolicyAuthValue failed (0x%x)\n", rv);
+	}
+	if (rv == TPM_SUCCESS) {
+		rv = TlclReadPolicy(index, out, length, session, nonce_tpm,
+				    auth, VB2_SHA256_DIGEST_SIZE);
+		if (rv != TPM_SUCCESS)
+			printf("unlock: NV_Read failed (0x%x)\n", rv);
+	}
+
+	/* Sessions are a scarce resource in the TPM; never leak one. */
+	rv_flush = TlclFlushContext(session);
+	if (rv == TPM_SUCCESS && rv_flush != TPM_SUCCESS)
+		rv = rv_flush;
+
+	return rv;
+}
+
+/* Cap PCR12 so the booted system cannot read the index again. Runs on every
+ * path, including the ones where the unlock failed: a machine that gave up on
+ * the PIN must not hand the next stage a still-open door. */
+static void unlock_cap_pcr(void)
+{
+	uint8_t digest[VB2_SHA256_DIGEST_SIZE];
+	uint8_t out[VB2_SHA256_DIGEST_SIZE];
+	const char marker[] = "swifty-unlock-done";
+
+	if (vb2_digest_buffer((const uint8_t *)marker, sizeof(marker) - 1,
+			      VB2_HASH_SHA256, digest, sizeof(digest)))
+		memset(digest, 0xff, sizeof(digest));
+
+	if (TlclExtend(UNLOCK_PCR_CAP, digest, out) != TPM_SUCCESS)
+		printf("unlock: WARNING: could not cap PCR%d\n",
+		       UNLOCK_PCR_CAP);
+}
+
+/*
+ * Returns 0 when the keys were published. A non-zero return is not fatal on
+ * its own: the caller decides, and an unencrypted volume still boots.
+ */
+int unlock_disk_keys(uint32_t index, uint32_t key_count)
+{
+	struct unlock_keys_header *block;
+	char pin[UNLOCK_PIN_MAX];
+	uint8_t auth[VB2_SHA256_DIGEST_SIZE];
+	size_t size;
+	uint32_t rv;
+	const char *note = NULL;
+	int tries;
+	int ret = -1;
+
+	size = sizeof(*block) + key_count * sizeof(struct unlock_key);
+	block = unlock_alloc_block(size);
+	if (!block) {
+		printf("unlock: no memory for the key block\n");
+		unlock_cap_pcr();
+		return -1;
+	}
+
+	unlock_screen_begin();
+	printf("\nswifty: unlock the disk.\n");
+
+	for (tries = UNLOCK_PIN_TRIES; tries > 0; tries--) {
+		if (tries < UNLOCK_PIN_TRIES)
+			note = "That PIN was not accepted. Try again.";
+		if (unlock_prompt_pin(pin, sizeof(pin), note)) {
+			/* Escape, or an empty line: the person at the keyboard
+			 * has said no, so stop asking. */
+			printf("unlock: no PIN entered\n");
+			break;
+		}
+
+		if (unlock_auth_value(pin, auth)) {
+			printf("unlock: could not derive the auth value\n");
+			break;
+		}
+
+		rv = unlock_read_index(index, auth, block, size);
+		memset(pin, 0, sizeof(pin));
+		memset(auth, 0, sizeof(auth));
+
+		if (rv == TPM_SUCCESS) {
+			ret = 0;
+			break;
+		}
+
+		/* Deliberately vague: a wrong PIN and a wrong machine should
+		 * look the same from the outside. */
+		printf("unlock: the TPM did not release the keys (0x%x), "
+		       "%d left\n", rv, tries - 1);
+	}
+
+	if (!ret && (block->total_keys == 0 || block->total_keys > key_count)) {
+		printf("unlock: key block says %u keys\n", block->total_keys);
+		ret = -1;
+	}
+
+	if (!ret)
+		printf("unlock: %u key(s) handed to the kernel\n",
+		       block->total_keys);
+
+	/* The PIN and the auth value have no business outliving this call. */
+	memset(pin, 0, sizeof(pin));
+	memset(auth, 0, sizeof(auth));
+	if (ret)
+		memset(block, 0, size);
+	unlock_screen_end();
+	unlock_cap_pcr();
+
+	return ret;
+}
diff --git a/src/vboot/unlock.h b/src/vboot/unlock.h
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..9014073
--- /dev/null
+++ b/src/vboot/unlock.h
@@ -0,0 +1,34 @@
+/* SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0 */
+/*
+ * Firmware unlock: the disk keys, out of the Cr50 and into the kernel.
+ * See src/vboot/unlock.c.
+ */
+
+#ifndef __VBOOT_UNLOCK_H__
+#define __VBOOT_UNLOCK_H__
+
+#include <stdint.h>
+
+/*
+ * The NV index the keys live in, and how many of them it holds. Both are
+ * fixed here rather than discovered: swifty-enroll defines the index at
+ * exactly the size this key count implies, and a firmware that disagreed
+ * with it would fail the read with nothing useful to say.
+ *
+ * Note the form. tpm2-tools and swifty-enroll name this index 0x01500001,
+ * with the TPM_HT_NV_INDEX byte on the front. vboot's TLCL takes the BARE
+ * index and adds that byte itself, as every other Tcl* call in it does, so
+ * the bare number is what belongs here. Writing the full handle asks the TPM
+ * for 0x02500001, which it refuses with TPM_RC_VALUE.
+ */
+#define UNLOCK_NV_INDEX		0x00500001
+#define UNLOCK_NV_KEY_COUNT	2
+
+/*
+ * Ask for the PIN, read the keys, publish them for the kernel, and cap PCR12.
+ * Returns 0 when the keys were published. A non-zero return is not fatal on
+ * its own -- the caller decides -- and PCR12 is capped either way.
+ */
+int unlock_disk_keys(uint32_t index, uint32_t key_count);
+
+#endif /* __VBOOT_UNLOCK_H__ */
